  "tour": {
    "title": "The Divine Council: From the Old Testament to the New",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the basic idea—God’s heavenly administration and the spiritual beings around him—then traces the worldview through Job, Psalm 82, and Deuteronomy 32. From there, it follows the thread into Paul’s language about invisible powers and into Revelation’s use of Daniel’s heavenly courtroom, so you can hear not just what the worldview is but why it changes how you read the New Testament.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e3-spiritual-warfare",
        "startSec": 400,
        "endSec": 580,
        "why": "Begin here for the vocabulary and the basic picture. The speakers explain that biblical authors can speak of other “Elohim” without putting them on Yahweh’s level, then define the divine council as God “like a commander in chief of a staff of Elohim,” illustrating it with Yahweh enthroned among the heavenly host in 1 Kings 22.",
        "episodeTitle": "Spiritual Warfare",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387",
        "durationSec": 4363,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:wisdom-series-job-part-2",
        "startSec": 600,
        "endSec": 700,
        "why": "This gives you an Old Testament narrative that assumes the worldview rather than merely naming it. Job’s opening scene is described as “God presiding over the sons of God in his divine court,” an image of God’s “decision-making room” that the speakers connect with the council scenes in 1 Kings and Zechariah.",
        "episodeTitle": "Job E2: Where on Earth is \"Uz\"?",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/9158c4f9-a010-4d2f-b813-50405d6cd4c2/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=9158c4f9-a010-4d2f-b813-50405d6cd4c2",
        "durationSec": 2491,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e4-origin-spiritual-beings-mini-qr",
        "startSec": 2560,
        "endSec": 2740,
        "why": "Now hear the clearest named example. The speakers read Psalm 82: “God takes his stand in the council of El,” explain that “the phrase divine council is actually a biblical phrase,” and interpret the addressed “Elohim” as delegated spiritual beings whom God confronts and judges.",
        "episodeTitle": "Origin of Spiritual Beings + Mini Question & Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/d7e45e15-6a73-4ddc-a3a7-42e0547ef789/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=d7e45e15-6a73-4ddc-a3a7-42e0547ef789",
        "durationSec": 4082,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:moses-final-words",
        "startSec": 1990,
        "endSec": 2170,
        "why": "This widens the picture from a heavenly courtroom to the nations. The discussion connects Deuteronomy 32 with the “table of 70 nations” and God handing the nations over to the sons of Elohim, while also honestly noting the manuscript difference and the speakers’ reservation about treating the textual question too simply. It lands on the crucial contrast: “Yahweh’s inheritance is his special people.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Deuteronomy: Moses’ Final Words",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/018604b1-78df-4140-81f5-4c570d6cdfd1/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=018604b1-78df-4140-81f5-4c570d6cdfd1",
        "durationSec": 4759,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6c2d7a78-com-857189b5-f81e-57f0-bb3a-ee0f68ade8b1",
        "startSec": 300,
        "endSec": 470,
        "why": "This is the bridge into the New Testament’s christology. The commentator explains that “thrones, dominions, rulers, or authorities” includes supernatural powers in the ancient context, and that Jesus is not merely one created being among them: “he is the ruler of all of creation” and all things were made “through him and for him.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Colossians 1:15-23",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/3j8tqp/1_15-23.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1490,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:443675d9-com-1c505f52-8ecf-31f4-be17-93eac135f7c3",
        "startSec": 570,
        "endSec": 750,
        "why": "Hear what the worldview does in Christian practice and in Paul’s reading of the unseen world. The speaker connects Ephesians’ rulers and authorities with “spiritual beings of wickedness in the heavenly places,” but emphasizes that Jesus is exalted “far above” them and that the church has “new power to live differently.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Ephesians 6:10-17",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/v54f7g/ephesians_610-1763dzi.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1706,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:3003",
        "startSec": 2010,
        "endSec": 2190,
        "why": "Finish by seeing why this matters for reading apocalyptic New Testament texts. The speaker explains the council’s roles—attendants, participants, messengers, and advocates—and insists that Daniel 7 “is going to be the backdrop” for Revelation 4–5, which is therefore “very clearly a divine council courtroom scene.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 366: Revelation 4 Part 1",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/NakedBible366_Revelation4_Part1.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3221,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You have now heard the council as a recurring biblical pattern: Yahweh rules amid a heavenly host, spiritual rulers can rebel and be judged, Israel is chosen amid the nations, and Jesus is presented as creator and supreme ruler over every visible and invisible power. When you meet “rulers,” “authorities,” heavenly assemblies, thrones, or courtroom scenes in the New Testament, you can ask what Old Testament council scene the writer expects you to recognize.",
    "totalSeconds": 1170
  },
